 "Same dry-season numbers as Cartagena - 30-32C, sea at 28-29C, almost no rain - but on Barú the wind is not a footnote, it shapes the days. December to May the trade winds blow hard over this stretch and the practical effect is that mornings are glassy while afternoons get choppy, with local guides describing afternoon boat crossings in this season as genuinely challenging. Three consequences: book every boat activity for the morning; expect Playa Blanca's water to turn from turquoise to stirred-up and greenish on the windiest afternoons, sometimes with seaweed; and know that the wind masks the sun, so people burn badly here without noticing. The upside is real - it is the driest, clearest, least-mosquito time of year, with 11-plus hours of sun a day. Early March also sits clear of Semana Santa (Palm Sunday 21 March 2027), which on Barú means the difference between a quiet island and a Colombian domestic-holiday crush.": "",
