 "The Keys run 1-2C warmer than Miami in early March: highs around 27-28C, lows 21-22C, water about 24C, and it is the dry season so rain is minimal. What actually matters is WIND. Late-winter cold fronts sweep through every 5-10 days bringing 15-25kt northerlies for two or three days, which chops up the ocean side, kills reef visibility and gets snorkel and charter trips cancelled. Practical rule: book the boat day for the calmest forecast day of your four, keep a spare day behind it, and hold the bayside kayak plan as the wind-proof backup. Bring long sleeves — an evening on a boat in a March front is genuinely cold.": "",
 "The Roma Sur market that serves the Colombian, Cuban and Venezuelan communities: arepas, Oaxacan cheese counters, mole pastes by the kilo, flower stalls, cheap ceviche and a dozen fondas doing comida corrida. Not a tourist market.": "",
 "The architectural splurge: five bedrooms, rooftop, sauna and a copper soaking bath.": "",
 "The architecture pick: Godefroy's board-formed concrete, cisterns and shade, Glou Glou wine bar.": "",
 "The best-designed mid-size house in Barranco; Ananay's restoration is genuinely good.": "",
 "The big day, and the reason Barú works. Charter your own speedboat rather than joining a day tour: a 28ft (10-12 pax) runs from 1,800,000 COP, about USD 450, and a 29ft from about USD 475, captain and fuel included, cooler and sound system on board. Split five ways that is USD 90-95 each - better than the $93-125pp beach-club day passes and you control the route and the clock. Standard run: leave around 08:30 while the sea is still flat, snorkel the Rosario reefs (10-15m visibility, protected marine park), lunch at an island club, then Cholón in the afternoon - the anchored-boat lagoon where everyone rafts up, sound systems competing, floating bars selling ceviche and mojitos. Loud, messy, very Colombian, and far better on a boat you control. Add USD 7.50pp national park fee and USD 2pp maritime insurance, cash only.": "",
 "The big hilltop fortress with the tunnel network. Go at 16:00-17:30, not midday - there is no shade and March sun is punishing. About an hour and a half including the tunnels and the view back over the old city.": "",
 "The cobalt-blue house on Londres 247 where Kahlo was born, lived with Rivera and died, with her studio, wheelchair at the easel, corsets and the garden pyramid. Pair it with the Coyoacan centre - Mercado de Coyoacan for tostadas, Jardin Centenario, and the Museo Casa Leon Trotsky four blocks away.": "",
